1
00:00:00,420 --> 00:00:05,845
The CLI and API in this synthetic order
service both validate quantity,

2
00:00:05,925 --> 00:00:08,250
and the rule is duplicated.

3
00:00:08,330 --> 00:00:12,793
Issue thirty-one asks us to share the
validator and keep the behavior of both

4
00:00:12,846 --> 00:00:13,408
callers.

5
00:00:14,053 --> 00:00:18,997
Open the issue and point to the expected
outputs before asking for edits.

6
00:00:19,050 --> 00:00:23,120
We want a small structural change with
the same results for the same inputs.

7
00:00:25,136 --> 00:00:27,328
Run the existing suite first.

8
00:00:27,381 --> 00:00:30,534
The synthetic baseline shows eight
passing tests,

9
00:00:30,588 --> 00:00:33,788
including normal and boundary cases
around the two callers.

10
00:00:34,454 --> 00:00:38,035
Capture the starting commit and command
with that result.

11
00:00:38,115 --> 00:00:41,482
This gives us a comparison point after
each stage.

12
00:00:41,562 --> 00:00:45,517
If a boundary isn't already covered,
agree on how to capture it before you

13
00:00:45,571 --> 00:00:47,261
ask the agent to move the rule.

14
00:00:49,489 --> 00:00:54,833
Ask the agent to map the two callers and
propose one stage for each migration.

15
00:00:54,887 --> 00:00:58,688
The first stage extracts the rule and
points the CLI at it.

16
00:00:59,328 --> 00:01:02,161
The second stage changes the API.

17
00:01:02,241 --> 00:01:06,276
Check that the agent can name the files
and validation command.

18
00:01:06,356 --> 00:01:10,418
Then approve the first stage and leave
the second stage waiting for the diff

19
00:01:10,498 --> 00:01:11,637
and test evidence.

20
00:01:13,864 --> 00:01:18,113
Look at Git status first so a newly
created file is visible.

21
00:01:18,193 --> 00:01:23,427
In the example, the shared validator and
the CLI are the only files changed.

22
00:01:24,072 --> 00:01:28,214
Open the validator and check the
comparison, then follow the call in the

23
00:01:28,294 --> 00:01:29,844
CLI.

24
00:01:29,898 --> 00:01:33,970
The tests should still show the same
behavior for quantity one, zero,

25
00:01:34,024 --> 00:01:35,224
and negative values.

26
00:01:35,914 --> 00:01:38,710
Review that evidence before the API
stage.

27
00:01:40,920 --> 00:01:42,844
Here's the staged handoff.

28
00:01:42,898 --> 00:01:46,906
The agent proposes opening the API and a
deployment file.

29
00:01:46,986 --> 00:01:49,258
Pause on the deployment entry.

30
00:01:49,311 --> 00:01:52,464
The issue names an API caller and a
behavior check,

31
00:01:52,518 --> 00:01:55,368
so there is no reason to approve that
file in this step.

32
00:01:56,023 --> 00:02:00,940
Tell the agent which call site belongs
to the scope and ask for a revised plan

33
00:02:00,993 --> 00:02:02,832
before it edits anything further.

34
00:02:05,110 --> 00:02:10,000
After the API stage, confirm the names
of all three changed files and inspect

35
00:02:10,027 --> 00:02:11,416
the new call.

36
00:02:11,496 --> 00:02:14,701
Run the same test command again, on the
final state.

37
00:02:15,368 --> 00:02:17,746
Here all eight tests still pass.

38
00:02:17,800 --> 00:02:21,594
Compare the observed boundary cases with
the baseline and check the public

39
00:02:21,648 --> 00:02:22,850
outputs.

40
00:02:22,904 --> 00:02:26,053
The final run is the one that supports
this exact diff.

41
00:02:28,249 --> 00:02:32,792
The pull request can now explain what
changed, what stayed compatible,

42
00:02:32,899 --> 00:02:34,529
and how we checked it.

43
00:02:34,609 --> 00:02:38,376
Include both runs and the scope decision
about the deployment file.

44
00:02:39,031 --> 00:02:43,627
A separate human reviewer can inspect
the diff and check status using GitHub

45
00:02:43,707 --> 00:02:46,968
CLI from the authorized terminal.

46
00:02:47,021 --> 00:02:50,869
They can follow the same evidence trail
without having to watch your agent

47
00:02:50,923 --> 00:02:51,381
session.

48
00:02:53,401 --> 00:02:57,820
For practice, find a second repeated
rule in the training checkout.

49
00:02:57,901 --> 00:03:01,642
Decide what the first stage proves and
what the second stage proves.

50
00:03:02,308 --> 00:03:06,129
Keep both stages small enough to explain
in a sentence.

51
00:03:06,209 --> 00:03:11,121
If you can point to a baseline, a stop
point, and a final independent check,

52
00:03:11,174 --> 00:03:13,661
you have a refactoring workflow you can
reuse.
